with (ROOT/'CORRECTIONS.tsv').open('a',newline='') as f:
 w=csv.writer(f,delimiter='\t')
 w.writerow(['sources/fr/fr/195.tex','French corrected','195-384','remark base letter','C.3 remark','Y','S','The scan prints Y. The object is X over S and U must be an open neighbourhood in S over which X has a section; there is no Y in this discussion. Existing correction confirmed.','misprint of the original','low'])
 w.writerow(['sources/fr/fr/195.tex','French corrected','195-384','proposition unclosed parenthesis','C.3 Proposition 3,1',r'O_S. Alors',r'O_S). Alors','The scan opens a prose parenthesis at si f est propre but never closes it. Its explanation ends after f_*O_X=O_S, before the main conclusion Alors. Existing closing parenthesis confirmed.','misprint of the original','low'])
event('195-C3-Picard-group','targeted proof check','Tensor product, dual, and the trivial line bundle give natural multiplication, inverse, and identity on the represented Picard functor. Yoneda produces the group-scheme structure, and the identities follow from the corresponding identities on every test scheme. The identification with generalized Jacobians of appropriate singular complete curves is a background identification requiring the curve/modulus construction, not proved here.','195, p. 384, first paragraph')
event('195-3.1-bare-hypotheses','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement id: 195-3.1-with-local-section. If the displayed proposition is read without the surrounding local-section assumption, take S=Spec(R) and the smooth projective conic x^2+y^2+z^2=0. It is flat and has universally f_*O_X=O_S. Over C it is P1, and O(1) gives a Picard class fixed by the two pullbacks to C tensor_R C=C times C. It cannot descend to a line bundle on X_R: every closed point has degree two, so every divisor and hence every line bundle on this regular curve has even degree. Thus F(R) to F(C) double-arrow F(C tensor_R C) is not an equalizer, although R to C double-arrow C tensor_R C is. A pro-representable algebra functor preserves that equalizer. On field spectra the printed Zariski definition is the actual Picard group, so the counterexample applies to it. The preceding local-section convention may be intended to govern the proposition; no hypothesis is silently inserted into either French layer.','195, p. 384, Proposition 3,1, literal hypothesis list')
event('195-3.1-with-local-section','targeted proof check','With a section locally on the base and universal f_*O_X=O_S, a line bundle can be rigidified by a generating section of its restriction to that section after localizing the base. Its automorphisms are base units, and the rigidification forces the unit to be one. Hence the descent isomorphisms, when they exist, are unique and satisfy the cocycle relation. Combining this with the invertible-module descent input of B.2 and the criterion B.1 supplies the intended pro-representability route. This check verifies the removal of the automorphism obstruction, not an independent proof of the general B.2 descent theorem. An arbitrary nongenerating marked section would not suffice.','195, p. 384, rigidification paragraph and Proposition 3,1 with preceding section convention')
event('195-C3-Picard-tangent','targeted proof check','Twisting by the pullback of the chosen line bundle reduces its split dual-number deformation problem to the trivial class. The units sequence has kernel 1+epsilon O, canonically the additive sheaf O. The splitting over A lifts every global unit, so its long exact sequence identifies the Picard kernel with H^1(X_A,O_X_A). Thus the printed exponent one is correct here. At finite residue fields, properness gives finite-dimensional H^1; conditional on pro-representability, A.5,1 gives noetherian local components.','195, p. 384, tangent display and corollary')
event('195-C3-base-change-parenthesis','carried as background without fault found','Over a field the tensor-product computation of H^0 gives the stated universal base-change implication for quasi-compact separated schemes, in particular proper ones. The printed additional proper/separable-fibre route invokes cohomological base change; its exact historical hypotheses and the later author erratum require a separate note. Restored the original wording propre et à fibres séparables in both exposé layers without merging the erratum.','195, p. 384, parenthesis in Proposition 3,1')
event('195-C3-principal-bundles','targeted proof check','For a principal G-bundle P, automorphisms are sections of the conjugation twist P times^G G. If G is commutative, conjugation is trivial and the adjoint group is canonically G over X. For noncommutative G it can be a nontrivial inner form, so the same scalar-rigidification argument does not automatically apply. The phrase n\'est plus trivial is read as loss of the general triviality guarantee, not as a universal claim even for the trivial torsor. The proposed general moduli construction is not independently proved.','195, p. 384, final remarks')
record_page('195',384,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/195.tex entry lines 738–790 and sources/fr/fr/195.tex entry lines 739–791 with scan leaf 17 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands. Checked Y/S in the remark, the proposition's inline direct-image formula, each nested subscript and star, the missing prose closing parenthesis, and the tangent exponent 1.

Both layers remove two unprinted editorial footnotes, restore the original propre et à fibres séparables (the author erratum is not merged), comma numbering, the opening colon, ROSENLICHT capitals, selective emphasis, wavy O/L/P and upright variables. The topological coefficient ring O stays plain. The first equality is inline; the tangent equality has no terminal comma. Printed French retains Y and the missing closing parenthesis; corrected French retains S and the necessary parenthesis, with explicit evidence. The tangent H^1 is retained in both layers.

Derived the group structure by Yoneda and the tangent formula by the split units sequence. Verified the rigidification mechanism with a generating marked section and universal direct-image equality. A real conic supplies an explicit equalizer failure if Proposition 3,1 is read without the surrounding local-section assumption; this qualification is recorded rather than inserted into the source. The generalized-Jacobian identification, general descent criterion and precise cohomological base-change theorem remain named inputs rather than claimed independent proofs. Checked the adjoint-group explanation of the noncommutative obstruction.''')
